  "answer": "I'll start by locating the file and finding the `shift` function.\nThe bug is at line 1602: `table[key + 1]`. Let me read the surrounding context with a line window to confirm the intended semantics before editing.\nThe file is 1602 lines (mostly generated `handler*` exports), and `shift` is the final line. Before editing, let me verify `table[key + 1]` appears only once so the targeted edit is unambiguous.\nOnly one occurrence. Applying the minimal fix — `table[key + 1]` → `table[key]` — with the fingerprint from my read:\nThe fix is applied. Now let me check the test setup and run the test suite:\nThe test uses `bun:test`, so the runner is Bun. Running the test suite:\nTest passes. Let me do a final verification of the edited line:\nFixed and verified. Summary:\n\n**The bug** — In `src/registry.ts` (1602 lines, mostly generated `handler*` exports), the `shift` function on the final line returned the wrong element:\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key + 1] }\n```\n\n**The fix** — I confirmed via grep that `table[key + 1]` occurred exactly once, then changed only the lookup expression:\n```ts\nexport function shift(table: number[], key: number) { return table[key] }\n```\n\n**Scope** — One expression on one line changed; the function signature and all ~1600 other exports (`handler1` … `handler1599`) are untouched, as are `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, and `test/oracle.test.ts`.\n\n**Test run** — `bun test` (the project's existing `bun:test` runner):\n```\n(pass) lookup at the key [0.56ms]\n 1 pass, 0 fail, 3 expect() calls\n```\nThe passing assertions confirm the semantics: `shift([10, 20, 30], 0)` now returns `10` (previously `20`) and `shift([10, 20, 30], 2)` returns `30` (previously `undefined`, an out-of-bounds read at index 3)."
